Enable rockchip-suspend on MiniLoong Pocket1 so the board can wake again.

rk3568.dtsi defines rockchip_suspend with status = "disabled", and nothing in
the MiniLoong DTS enables it. The PM driver therefore starts with no sleep-mode
or wakeup configuration and says so at boot:

  rockchip-suspend: not set sleep-mode-config

A power-key press then suspends into a state with no configured exit path. The
board looks like it powered off and never comes back - SSH drops and only a
forced power-off recovers it.

The defaults already in rk3568.dtsi suit this board. dArkOS ships exactly the
same values - sleep-mode-config 0x5ec (ARMOFF_LOGOFF | CENTER_OFF | HW_PLLS_OFF
| PMUALIVE_32K | OSC_DIS | PMIC_LP | 32K_PVTM) and wakeup-config 0x10
(GPIO_WKUP_EN) - and only flips the status, so do the same rather than
duplicating the values here. The rk805 PMIC power key is already status =
"okay" and can wake the SoC once a sleep mode is actually configured.
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-miniloong.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-miniloong.dts
@@ -1391,6 +1391,21 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * rk3568.dtsi ships this node disabled. Without it the PM driver never gets a
+ * sleep-mode or wakeup configuration - the kernel says "rockchip-suspend not
+ * set sleep-mode-config" at boot - so a suspend request puts the SoC into a
+ * state it has no configured way out of: the board appears to power off on a
+ * power-key press and never wakes.
+ *
+ * The defaults in rk3568.dtsi are already right for this board; dArkOS ships
+ * the identical values (sleep-mode-config 0x5ec, wakeup-config 0x10) and only
+ * flips the status, so do the same rather than restating them here.
+ */
+&rockchip_suspend {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 &rk_rga {
 	status = "okay";
 };
